============================================================
THE RAPIDITY CIRCLE OF FIFTHS
============================================================

  In music: the circle of fifths stacks 12 fifths to return
  (approximately) to the starting note, off by a Pythagorean comma.

  In rapidity: stacking k fifths means adding k * ln(3/2)/2.
  After 12 fifths: 12 * ln(3/2)/2 = 7 * ln(2)/2 + comma/2

  The circle of fifths in rapidity (mod octave = mod ln(2)/2):

   0 fifths: rapidity =  0.00000, mod octave = 0.00000, ~ C
   1 fifths: rapidity =  0.20273, mod octave = 0.20273, ~ G
   2 fifths: rapidity =  0.40547, mod octave = 0.05889, ~ D
   3 fifths: rapidity =  0.60820, mod octave = 0.26162, ~ A
   4 fifths: rapidity =  0.81093, mod octave = 0.11778, ~ E
   5 fifths: rapidity =  1.01366, mod octave = 0.32052, ~ B
   6 fifths: rapidity =  1.21640, mod octave = 0.17667, ~ F#
   7 fifths: rapidity =  1.41913, mod octave = 0.03283, ~ C#
   8 fifths: rapidity =  1.62186, mod octave = 0.23557, ~ G#
   9 fifths: rapidity =  1.82459, mod octave = 0.09173, ~ D#
  10 fifths: rapidity =  2.02733, mod octave = 0.29446, ~ A#
  11 fifths: rapidity =  2.23006, mod octave = 0.15062, ~ F
  12 fifths: rapidity =  2.43279, mod octave = 0.00678, ~ C

  After 12 fifths, the rapidity doesn't return to 0 (mod octave).
  The excess = Pythagorean comma = 12*r_3 - 19*r_2 = 0.006776
  This is the fundamental OBSTRUCTION to closing the circle.

============================================================
TOURNAMENT THEORY AS MUSIC THEORY: THE FULL DICTIONARY
============================================================

  MUSIC                  TOURNAMENT               FORMULA
  ------------------------------------------------------------
  note                   H-value                  H(T)
  octave                 doubling H               2*H
  interval               H-ratio                  H1/H2
  consonance             small rapidity diff      |ln(H1/H2)|/2
  chord                  set of tournaments       {T: H(T) in S}
  scale                  H-spectrum at n          {H(T): T on n}
  tuning                 choice of weights        independence poly eval
  comma                  near-coincidence         H1/H2 ~ 1
  circle of fifths       iterating Q(1/5)         (3/2)^k mod octave
  Pythagorean comma      12 fifths != 7 octaves   (3/2)^12 != 2^7
  just intonation        superparticular H-ratios (n+1)/n
  equal temperament      regular rapidity grid    exp(k*ln(2)/24)
  overtone series        prime factorization      H = prod p^{a_p}
  fundamental            smallest prime factor    min prime of H
  harmonic               prime factor             p | H
  dissonance             large prime factor       large p | H

  The FORBIDDEN H-values {7, 21} are the 4th and 6th HARMONICS
  of the curvature quantum 3:
  7 = the 4th harmonic in the odd series (1, 3, 5, 7)
  21 = 3 * 7 = fundamental * 4th harmonic
  They are DISSONANT with every achievable H-value.

  In music, the 7th harmonic (Bb) and 11th harmonic (F#/4)
  are the first 'wrong-sounding' overtones in Western tuning.
  Our forbidden numbers are the 'wrong-sounding' path counts.
